;; Reviewed 2026-10-09 (written by Claude, from the design, the MACRO
;; manual, LLVM's code generator and the guest runs; for the user's
;; review). Why this is right:
;; - Phase 16 step 3 (section 14, item 11): ASSERT(x) branches on x as IF
;;   does (JumpOn: BLBS, a compare and a signed branch, & and OR short-
;;   circuit, each inverted branch over JMP L^), to a call of POC_TRAP at
;;   the ASSERT statement's line and column, as LLVM's statement trap.
;; - The code is 10, LLVM's status for a failed assertion; ASSERT(x, n)
;;   puts n + 1 in its high word, ^X0001000A for 0, ^X0008000A for 7,
;;   ^X0100000A for 255, so that POC_TRAP writes "assertion failed (n)"
;;   with $FAO, and "assertion failed" for 10 alone.
;; - ASSERT(TRUE) jumps over its trap, as LLVM evaluates it and branches.
;; - Asserts, run on the guest with the real runtime for each case, prints
;;   LLVM's messages and ends with %X10000052 where LLVM exits with 10
;;   (output.expected, plain.expected).
; VaxAssert, written by poc (doc/developer/vax-macro32-backend.md)
